# Workspace Workflows — Code-Free Standards

## 🎯 Principles

- Rules are code-free and outcome-focused; implementation details live in code and docs, not in rules.
- Favor simple, generic guidance that scales across languages and stacks.
- Anchor work to three recurring workflows: restructure documentation, simplify programs, ensure operational readiness.

## 🔁 Core Workflows

- Restructuring Documentation

  - Audit existing docs; remove duplication; split by concern (architecture, interfaces, behavior, testing, guides, status).
  - Keep documents concise with clear acceptance criteria and links; avoid code snippets.
  - Maintain a SYSTEM_STATUS with completed/in-progress/planned and next steps.

- Simplification of Programs

  - Prefer clean architecture boundaries; reduce surface area; remove unused code and configuration.
  - Centralize configuration; standardize naming and interfaces; favor readability over cleverness.
  - Introduce or refine adapters and factories only when they simplify integration and testing.

- Operational Readiness
  - Provide launch/run instructions, validation checklists, and safety notes.
  - Ensure critical paths are testable; define minimal E2E flows and success criteria.
  - Track known issues and risks with clear mitigations and owners.

## ✅ Quality Gates (generic)

- Documentation present, concise, and accurate; no code in rules.
- Architecture boundaries respected; dependencies flow inward.
- Tests and validation steps exist for critical flows; status file updated.

## 🧩 Integration with Commands

- Explore → Plan → Code → Test → Iterate → Validate → Status.
- Each command should map to at least one workflow and update status/tasks accordingly.

---

This rule is generic and code-free; it guides how we structure work and decisions across projects.
